::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q09::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 8<sup>2/3</sup>? {
=(cuberoot(8))<sup>2</sup> #Correct.
~(sqrt(8))<sup>3</sup> #Not quite. The root and power are reversed.
~8<sup>2</sup>/3 #Not quite. The exponent is not division.
~8 + 2/3 #Not quite. The exponent is not addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q10::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 11<sup>4/5</sup>? {
~(fourthroot(11))<sup>5</sup> #Not quite. The root and power are reversed.
=(fifthroot(11))<sup>4</sup> #Correct.
~11<sup>5</sup>/4 #Not quite. The exponent is not division.
~11<sup>9/5</sup> #Not quite. The exponent was incorrectly changed.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q11::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 2<sup>1/4</sup> * 2<sup>3/4</sup>. {
~2<sup>3/16</sup> #Not quite. Exponents are added, not multiplied.
~4 #Not quite. The base was incorrectly changed.
=2 #Correct.
~2<sup>4/8</sup> #Not quite. The fraction was not simplified correctly.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q12::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 9<sup>5/2</sup> / 9<sup>1/2</sup>. {
~9<sup>3</sup> #Not quite. The exponents were not subtracted correctly.
~9<sup>6/2</sup> #Not quite. Exponents were added instead of subtracted.
~1 #Not quite. The exponents are not equal.
=9<sup>2</sup> #Correct.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q13::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 7<sup>2/3</sup> * 7<sup>4/3</sup>. {
=7<sup>2</sup> #Correct.
~7<sup>8/9</sup> #Not quite. Exponents are added, not multiplied.
~14<sup>6/3</sup> #Not quite. The base was incorrectly changed.
~7<sup>2/3</sup> #Not quite. One factor was ignored.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q14::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Simplify 10<sup>7/5</sup> / 10<sup>2/5</sup>. {
~10<sup>9/5</sup> #Not quite. Exponents were added instead of subtracted.
=10 #Correct.
~1 #Not quite. The exponents are not equal.
~10<sup>14/25</sup> #Not quite. Exponents were incorrectly multiplied.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q15::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] What does the denominator in 81<sup>3/4</sup> tell you to do? {
~Raise to the third power. #Not quite. This is the numeratorâ€™s role.
~Divide by 4. #Not quite. The denominator does not indicate division.
=Take the fourth root. #Correct.
~Multiply by 4. #Not quite. The denominator defines the root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q16::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] What does the numerator in 32<sup>2/5</sup> tell you to do? {
~Take the fifth root. #Not quite. This is the denominatorâ€™s role.
~Divide by 2. #Not quite. The numerator is not division.
~Multiply by 2. #Not quite. The numerator defines a power.
=Raise to the second power. #Correct.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q17::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] A student evaluates 64<sup>2/3</sup> as 64<sup>2</sup>/3. Which correction is best? {
=Use the cube root first, then apply the power. #Correct.
~Divide by 3, then square. #Not quite. The denominator defines a root.
~Square first and ignore the denominator. #Not quite. Both parts matter.
~Reverse the exponent. #Not quite. This changes the value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q18::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression has the same value as sqrt(50)? {
~50<sup>2</sup> #Not quite. This squares instead of taking a root.
=50<sup>1/2</sup> #Correct.
~50<sup>1/3</sup> #Not quite. This is a cube root.
~50/2 #Not quite. A root is not division.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q19::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression has the same value as cuberoot(19)? {
~19<sup>3</sup> #Not quite. This cubes instead of taking a root.
~19<sup>1/2</sup> #Not quite. This is a square root.
=19<sup>1/3</sup> #Correct.
~19/3 #Not quite. A root is not division.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q20::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to (4<sup>1/2</sup>)<sup>6</sup>? {
~4<sup>1/12</sup> #Not quite. Exponents are multiplied, not divided.
~24<sup>1/2</sup> #Not quite. The base was incorrectly changed.
~4<sup>6+1/2</sup> #Not quite. Exponents are multiplied, not added.
=4<sup>3</sup> #Correct.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q21::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 10000<sup>1/4</sup>. {
=10 #Correct.
~100 #Not quite. This uses a square root instead of a fourth root.
~2500 #Not quite. This treats the exponent as division.
~4 #Not quite. The denominator names the root, not the value.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q22::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Which expression is equivalent to 27<sup>4/3</sup>? {
~(fourthroot(27))<sup>3</sup> #Not quite. The root and power are reversed.
=(cuberoot(27))<sup>4</sup> #Correct.
~27<sup>4</sup>/3 #Not quite. The exponent is not division.
~27<sup>1/12</sup> #Not quite. The exponent was incorrectly changed.
}

::ALG1_U01_L01_Quiz_Q23::
[MA.912.NSO.1.1] Evaluate 27<sup>4/3</sup>. {
~3 #Not quite. Only the root was applied.
~36 #Not quite. The exponent was treated as multiplication.
=81 #Correct.
